2. Correct application of Common and Proper Nouns; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.
3. Correct application of Common and Proper Nouns; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.
4. Correct application of Common and Proper Nouns; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.
5. Correct application of Common and Proper Nouns; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.
6. Correct application of Common and Proper Nouns; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.

</details>

## Exit ticket

1. In one sentence, what does “Common and Proper Nouns” help a reader or writer do?
2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to quoting a line without explaining how…).
3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.

*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*

## Stretch (optional)

Find a paragraph in your own writing (or invent 6–8 sentences about a practice for the spring showcase). Annotate today’s grammar target, revise two places, and write a 3-sentence reflection on how clarity improved.

